    "finish_elapsed_s": 11.406971366028301,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, it absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen. As it cools, the refrigerant turns back into a liquid and cycles inside again. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold. The fridge doesn’t “create” cold—it just moves heat out, and insulation helps trap the cold air inside."
